\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {II}{\ignorespaces One worked example per question class, quoted from the released per-question outputs. A tick marks the recorded verdict; wrong answers are set in italic. None of these failures is a fluency failure: every wrong answer is well formed and plausible, and each substitutes the system's own prior for what the record states. Answers in the depth-aware column also carry the identifier of the record they were drawn from, omitted here for width. The first three rows are from the verified subset; the fourth is from a separate compositional evaluation, on which LINC was not run. That answer has three components and the systems differ on exactly one, shown in bold, so the cell lists the three components rather than the full sentence.}}{4}{table.2}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:examples}{{II}{4}{One worked example per question class, quoted from the released per-question outputs. A tick marks the recorded verdict; wrong answers are set in italic. None of these failures is a fluency failure: every wrong answer is well formed and plausible, and each substitutes the system's own prior for what the record states. Answers in the depth-aware column also carry the identifier of the record they were drawn from, omitted here for width. The first three rows are from the verified subset; the fourth is from a separate compositional evaluation, on which LINC was not run. That answer has three components and the systems differ on exactly one, shown in bold, so the cell lists the three components rather than the full sentence}{table.2}{}}
